"comparison": "Both AMD and Broadcom disclose standard redemption and change of control provisions for their senior notes. A key similarity is the specific repurchase price upon a change of control: both companies require paying 101% of the principal amount plus accrued interest (AMD: 'repurchase price of 101% of the principal amount plus accrued and unpaid interest'; AVGO: 'price equal to 101% of the principal amount of such notes, plus accrued and unpaid interest'). A key difference lies in the voluntary redemption mechanics: AMD uses a specific 'greater of' formula comparing present value of remaining payments or 100% of principal, whereas Broadcom explicitly references a 'specified make-whole premium determined in accordance with the indentures' in addition to accrued interest.",
